data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider blocked sync calls by default but let you opt back in with the sync-over-async flag (e.g., `CosmosOptionsExtension` / `EnableSyncOverAsync`). **In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), that opt-in is removed entirely.** All synchronous LINQ operators and `SaveChanges()` on the Cosmos DB provider throw `InvalidOperationException` unconditionally.

### Every call in your repository breaks:

| Sync call (breaks) | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `await .ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `await .FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `await .CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `await .SaveChangesAsync()` |

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `Add()` remains synchronous (it only tracks the entity in memory).
- Callers of these methods must also be updated to `await` — async propagates up the call chain.
- Remove any `EnableSyncOverAsync` / sync-over-async configuration; it's now a no-op or compile error.
